            <ArticleTitle>Spontaneous Involution of a Rathke Cleft Cyst.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e791-e793</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/SCS.0000000000003132</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Rathke cleft cysts (RCCs) are nonneoplastic lesions that are thought to be the remnants of Rathke cleft pouch. The authors report a patient presented with a headache and was diagnosed with RCC on imaging. The lesion underwent spontaneous involution. The authors suggest that patients presenting solely with a headache to be treated conservatively, because it is uncertain whether a headache is definitively associated with RCCs and because there is the possibility of spontaneous regression.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
